Z||000008||Nick||08-27-99||01:09 PM||||Well I don't work for Honda, but I do own a CRX Si, and they are NOT slow!  It's all in the driver with those cars.  You really have to know what you're doing in order to get the full potential out of it.  It is easier to get my 86 GT to go fast.  If it was a drag race with two professional drivers, it would be VERY close.  Just look at all the performance numbers on both cars.  They were pretty close, and I would imagine that tires and weather conditions and a lot of other things would play a big factor in who would win.  <p>As far as the twisties go, I really can't say because I've never had the balls to take the Fiero to the edge of what it can do.  I spent a lot of time and effort keeping it in mint condition and I wasn't about to risk losing it just to find out how well it corners.  
Z||000009||ChadMan||08-27-99||03:36 PM||||I think Nick's right.  I race a lot of Civics and CRX's with my 4-banger.  If the Honda driver winds it out to redline, he usually wins.  If he shifts earlier, I win.  It's usually pretty close either way.  And it's a great feeling to hear him shift at 5K, popping the clutch, chirping his tires, and wondering why he's still losing.<p>Of course, if I race them in the V6 car, it really doesn't matter what they do.  Only the VTEC Preludes are a threat.
